1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,535
A monorepo, one repository that holds several
projects, with a Python service and a

2
00:00:09,575 --> 00:00:14,609
Java service. Each has its own workflow,
the file that tells GitHub Actions what to

3
00:00:14,649 --> 00:00:19,378
run and when. Each workflow has a paths
filter, so that a documentation change

4
00:00:19,418 --> 00:00:22,129
doesn't spend twenty
runner minutes on Maven.

5
00:00:22,469 --> 00:00:27,438
Sensible. Then someone makes the Java
workflow a required check, one that a rule

6
00:00:27,478 --> 00:00:32,448
says must pass before a merge. The next
documentation pull request shows a check

7
00:00:32,488 --> 00:00:35,766
that's waiting, and it waits
until somebody gives up.

8
00:00:35,806 --> 00:00:37,520
Why? Take a guess, out loud.

9
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:47,118
Nothing failed. The filter did what it
was written to do: it decided that no run

10
00:00:47,158 --> 00:00:51,856
should exist. And a ruleset that requires
a check waits for a result that won't

11
00:00:51,896 --> 00:00:56,833
come. To predict that before you push, you
need to know how an event becomes a run,

12
00:00:56,873 --> 00:01:00,552
and that's this video. Keep that
waiting pull request in mind.

13
00:01:00,592 --> 00:01:02,591
You'll rebuild it in the terminal.

14
00:01:04,431 --> 00:01:07,719
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:07,759 --> 00:01:12,137
In the last video you learned the model,
and a reading order that starts with the

16
00:01:12,177 --> 00:01:16,610
trigger. Now you learn to read the trigger
properly, and then two more things that

17
00:01:16,650 --> 00:01:18,902
sit between the trigger and your commands.

18
00:01:19,242 --> 00:01:23,837
Three parts. Events and filters: which
commit and ref a run is about, which copy of

19
00:01:23,877 --> 00:01:28,472
the workflow file is used, and how a branch
or path filter decides whether a run is

20
00:01:28,512 --> 00:01:33,164
created at all. Contexts and expressions:
the data a workflow can read and the small

21
00:01:33,204 --> 00:01:37,576
formula language over it, with the question
that matters most, which is when an

22
00:01:37,616 --> 00:01:42,211
expression is evaluated. And env, vars and
secrets: three things that look alike in

23
00:01:42,251 --> 00:01:45,953
a script and differ in where they're
defined and who can read them.

24
00:01:46,293 --> 00:01:50,480
The path filter part has a local
demonstration, because the comparison GitHub

25
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:54,049
documents is a plain Git diff, and
you can run it before pushing.

26
00:01:55,889 --> 00:01:57,467
Here's what you'll walk away with.

27
00:01:57,807 --> 00:01:59,220
After this video you can:

28
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:04,045
say for push, pull_request, schedule and
workflow_dispatch which commit the run

29
00:02:04,085 --> 00:02:07,367
refers to and from which branch
the workflow file is read;

30
00:02:07,707 --> 00:02:12,202
predict whether a branch or path filter
starts a workflow for a given change;

31
00:02:12,542 --> 00:02:16,350
explain how changed paths are computed
for a pull request and for a push;

32
00:02:16,690 --> 00:02:19,302
read an expression and
say when it is evaluated;

33
00:02:19,642 --> 00:02:24,074
distinguish env, vars and secrets by where
they are defined and who can read them.

34
00:02:25,914 --> 00:02:30,319
Events. In one sentence: the on key
lists the events that start a run.

35
00:02:30,359 --> 00:02:35,145
Each event fixes which commit and ref
the run is about and which copy of the

36
00:02:35,185 --> 00:02:39,272
workflow file is used. A ref is a
name such as a branch or a tag.

37
00:02:39,312 --> 00:02:42,765
And filters narrow an event
to branches, tags or paths.

38
00:02:43,105 --> 00:02:47,159
on takes one event, a list, or a map
from event to its configuration.

39
00:02:47,199 --> 00:02:51,787
The textbook lists eight events with the
values the events reference gives for

40
00:02:51,827 --> 00:02:55,288
GITHUB_SHA and GITHUB_REF. Take
the four you'll meet first.

41
00:02:55,628 --> 00:03:00,395
push starts when commits or tags are pushed.
The commit is the tip commit pushed to

42
00:03:00,435 --> 00:03:05,259
the ref. The ref is the updated ref. It runs
for any branch, including workflows not

43
00:03:05,299 --> 00:03:07,055
merged into the default branch.

44
00:03:07,395 --> 00:03:12,715
pull_request starts when a pull request is
opened, gets new commits, or is reopened.

45
00:03:12,755 --> 00:03:17,500
Other activity types need types. The
commit is the last merge commit on the

46
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:20,819
GITHUB_REF branch, and the
ref is refs/pull/N/merge.

47
00:03:20,859 --> 00:03:24,902
That's the test merge that GitHub
computes for the pull request.

48
00:03:24,942 --> 00:03:29,241
So the commit isn't the tip of your
branch. Video 145 is about that.

49
00:03:29,281 --> 00:03:34,538
And the reference adds: it doesn't run while
the pull request has a merge conflict.

50
00:03:34,878 --> 00:03:39,515
workflow_dispatch starts when someone
starts it by hand, with the CLI or the API.

51
00:03:39,555 --> 00:03:44,019
The commit is the last commit on the
chosen branch or tag, and the ref is that

52
00:03:44,059 --> 00:03:48,581
branch or tag. It works only if the
workflow file exists on the default branch.

53
00:03:48,921 --> 00:03:54,992
schedule starts when a cron expression
matches. The commit is the last commit on the

54
00:03:55,032 --> 00:04:00,739
default branch. The ref is the default
branch. It's in UTC unless a timezone is

55
00:04:00,779 --> 00:04:06,560
given. The shortest interval is five
minutes. It can be delayed or dropped under

56
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:12,380
load. And in a public repository it is
disabled after 60 days without repository

57
00:04:12,420 --> 00:04:13,035
activity.

58
00:04:13,375 --> 00:04:18,105
The other four in the table are
workflow_call, workflow_run, merge_group and

59
00:04:18,145 --> 00:04:23,188
release. One remark on merge_group now,
because it's the same class of problem as

60
00:04:23,228 --> 00:04:28,460
the hook: required checks must listen to
it, or the merge queue never gets a result.

61
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:31,903
The textbook tells you to read
the table as three families.

62
00:04:31,943 --> 00:04:35,953
push, pull_request, merge_group and
release are about a specific commit that

63
00:04:35,993 --> 00:04:40,375
somebody produced. schedule and workflow_run
are about the default branch, whatever

64
00:04:40,415 --> 00:04:44,478
happened elsewhere. workflow_dispatch
is about whatever ref the person chose.

65
00:04:44,818 --> 00:04:49,022
One event is deliberately not in
the table: pull_request_target.

66
00:04:49,062 --> 00:04:54,593
It starts on the same pull request activity,
but it's about the base repository: the

67
00:04:54,633 --> 00:04:59,434
workflow file comes from the default
branch and the job receives the base

68
00:04:59,474 --> 00:05:04,806
repository's token and secrets. The
textbook's instruction is plain: don't use it

69
00:05:04,846 --> 00:05:07,989
before reading section
21A.5. That is video 157.

70
00:05:08,329 --> 00:05:13,183
Which copy of the workflow file. From the
last video: for push and pull_request the

71
00:05:13,223 --> 00:05:17,900
workflow definition is read from the commit
the event refers to, so a branch can

72
00:05:17,940 --> 00:05:22,852
change its own CI. For several other events
it is read from the default branch only.

73
00:05:23,192 --> 00:05:28,207
Filters. push accepts filters for branches,
tags and paths, each with an -ignore

74
00:05:28,247 --> 00:05:33,450
form. pull_request accepts the branch
filters, which match the base branch, and the

75
00:05:33,490 --> 00:05:35,283
two path filters. Four rules.

76
00:05:35,623 --> 00:05:40,201
A positive filter and an -ignore filter of
the same kind can't be combined for one

77
00:05:40,241 --> 00:05:44,706
event. You use an exclamation mark pattern
inside the positive filter, and order

78
00:05:44,746 --> 00:05:45,156
matters.

79
00:05:45,496 --> 00:05:50,729
When a branch filter and a path filter are
both present, the workflow runs only when

80
00:05:50,769 --> 00:05:51,922
both are satisfied.

81
00:05:52,262 --> 00:05:55,215
Path filters aren't evaluated
for pushes of tags.

82
00:05:55,555 --> 00:06:00,506
And the rule the demonstration is built
on: path filters compare with a three-dot

83
00:06:00,546 --> 00:06:03,772
diff for pull requests and
a two-dot diff for pushes.

84
00:06:03,812 --> 00:06:08,824
Two limits come with it. A push with more
than 1,000 commits, or a diff that times

85
00:06:08,864 --> 00:06:13,815
out, always runs the workflow. A diff of
more than 3,000 files in which the match

86
00:06:13,855 --> 00:06:16,033
isn't among the first 3,000 doesn't.

87
00:06:16,373 --> 00:06:21,400
Two more ways a run doesn't come to exist.
A commit whose message contains [skip

88
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:26,340
ci], or one of its documented variants,
skips push and pull_request workflows.

89
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:31,659
And events created by a workflow's own job
token don't start new workflow runs, with

90
00:06:31,699 --> 00:06:36,663
documented exceptions: workflow_dispatch,
repository_dispatch, and pull request

91
00:06:36,703 --> 00:06:40,716
events, which wait for approval
since the eleventh of June 2026.

92
00:06:40,756 --> 00:06:45,972
So a job that pushes a commit with the job
token doesn't trigger the push workflows

93
00:06:46,012 --> 00:06:46,986
for that commit.

94
00:06:47,326 --> 00:06:52,173
Contexts and expressions. In one sentence:
a context is a named object of facts

95
00:06:52,213 --> 00:06:57,371
about the run, and an expression, written
with a dollar sign and double braces, is a

96
00:06:57,411 --> 00:07:02,506
small formula over contexts that GitHub
Actions replaces with its value before your

97
00:07:02,546 --> 00:07:03,248
step starts.

98
00:07:03,588 --> 00:07:08,498
The contexts you'll use first: github, with
the event name, the full event payload,

99
00:07:08,538 --> 00:07:11,539
the ref, the commit, the
actor and the run numbers.

100
00:07:11,579 --> 00:07:16,369
env. vars. secrets. inputs. matrix. steps,
which holds the outputs and outcome of

101
00:07:16,409 --> 00:07:21,318
earlier steps that have an id. And needs,
which holds the outputs and result of the

102
00:07:21,358 --> 00:07:22,570
jobs listed in needs.

103
00:07:22,910 --> 00:07:25,973
The rules of the expression
language that cause surprises.

104
00:07:26,313 --> 00:07:30,759
Strings use single quotes inside the
braces. Double quotes are an error.

105
00:07:31,099 --> 00:07:36,001
String comparison ignores case. When the
two sides of an equality differ in type,

106
00:07:36,041 --> 00:07:37,832
both are converted to numbers.

107
00:07:38,172 --> 00:07:42,480
A property that doesn't exist evaluates
to an empty string, without an error.

108
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:45,868
A typing error in the name of
a step ID is therefore silent.

109
00:07:46,208 --> 00:07:51,731
The functions include contains, startsWith,
format, toJSON, fromJSON, hashFiles, and

110
00:07:51,771 --> 00:07:56,234
the status functions success(),
failure(), cancelled() and always().

111
00:07:56,274 --> 00:08:00,803
The function case has existed since
the twenty-ninth of January 2026.

112
00:08:00,843 --> 00:08:06,233
Older files imitate it with "condition and
a, or b", which returns b whenever a is

113
00:08:06,273 --> 00:08:06,630
falsy.

114
00:08:06,970 --> 00:08:12,449
Context availability is per key. A job-level
if can read github, needs, vars and

115
00:08:12,489 --> 00:08:17,899
inputs, but not matrix, steps, env or
secrets. A step-level if can read matrix,

116
00:08:17,939 --> 00:08:23,211
steps and env, but still not secrets.
The reason is timing: a job-level if is

117
00:08:23,251 --> 00:08:28,868
decided before a runner exists, so nothing
that lives on the runner can take part.

118
00:08:29,208 --> 00:08:34,145
The trap in if. The wrapper of dollar sign
and braces is optional in an if, unless

119
00:08:34,185 --> 00:08:39,122
the expression starts with an exclamation
mark. Text outside the wrapper turns the

120
00:08:39,162 --> 00:08:42,339
whole value into a non-empty
string, which is truthy.

121
00:08:42,379 --> 00:08:44,402
The condition is then always true.

122
00:08:44,742 --> 00:08:49,669
Since the twenty-ninth of January 2026 the
editor validation flags the third form

123
00:08:49,709 --> 00:08:51,631
and the run shows an annotation.

124
00:08:51,971 --> 00:08:56,650
env, vars and secrets. In one sentence:
env is defined in the workflow file and

125
00:08:56,690 --> 00:09:01,608
becomes environment variables of the process.
vars and secrets are stored in GitHub

126
00:09:01,648 --> 00:09:06,267
settings and are read through contexts.
Only secrets are encrypted and masked.

127
00:09:06,607 --> 00:09:11,232
Where each is defined: env in the workflow
file, at workflow, job or step level.

128
00:09:11,272 --> 00:09:15,546
vars and secrets in the settings of
the organization, the repository or an

129
00:09:15,586 --> 00:09:16,246
environment.

130
00:09:16,586 --> 00:09:21,134
Who can see it: env is in the file. vars
aren't in the repository but readable in

131
00:09:21,174 --> 00:09:24,192
settings and logs. secrets
are write-only in settings.

132
00:09:24,532 --> 00:09:27,827
How it reaches a step: env
as an environment variable.

133
00:09:27,867 --> 00:09:32,953
vars and secrets only through their context.
You map them into env, or for a secret

134
00:09:32,993 --> 00:09:34,003
into env or with.

135
00:09:34,343 --> 00:09:39,973
Precedence for the same name: for env, the
most specific wins, step over job over

136
00:09:40,013 --> 00:09:44,594
workflow. For vars, environment
over repository over organization.

137
00:09:44,634 --> 00:09:47,884
For secrets, the
environment-level secret wins.

138
00:09:48,224 --> 00:09:52,878
Masking: env no, vars no, secrets
yes, when the exact value appears.

139
00:09:52,918 --> 00:09:56,122
The textbook adds three
words: not a guarantee.

140
00:09:56,462 --> 00:10:00,791
Quick quiz. A deploy step reads
a secret that nobody ever set.

141
00:10:00,831 --> 00:10:04,948
What does the step receive? A,
an error that stops the run.

142
00:10:04,988 --> 00:10:07,203
B, an empty string. Your answer?

143
00:10:12,043 --> 00:10:15,606
B, an empty string. Three
behaviors cause real failures.

144
00:10:15,646 --> 00:10:20,753
An unset secret is an empty string, not
an error. A deploy step that receives an

145
00:10:20,793 --> 00:10:25,899
empty credential fails later and elsewhere,
with a message about authentication.

146
00:10:25,939 --> 00:10:31,303
Secrets aren't passed to workflows triggered
from forks or by Dependabot, apart from

147
00:10:31,343 --> 00:10:32,719
a read-only job token.

148
00:10:33,059 --> 00:10:36,973
A fork is a second repository
on GitHub, made from the first.

149
00:10:37,013 --> 00:10:42,159
A fork pull request that "fails only in
CI" is often a job that needed a secret.

150
00:10:42,199 --> 00:10:47,410
And secrets can't be used in if. You map
the secret into env and test whether the

151
00:10:47,450 --> 00:10:48,836
variable is non-empty.

152
00:10:49,176 --> 00:10:52,802
GITHUB_TOKEN is a secret that
GitHub creates for each job.

153
00:10:52,842 --> 00:10:57,794
What it may do is set by the permissions
key, and when any permission is named,

154
00:10:57,834 --> 00:10:59,753
every unnamed one becomes none.

155
00:11:01,593 --> 00:11:05,512
Analogy, from the textbook. An
expression is a mail-merge field.

156
00:11:05,552 --> 00:11:10,213
The letter, which is your step, is
printed with the field already filled in.

157
00:11:10,253 --> 00:11:14,605
The recipient, which is the shell,
never sees the field, only the text.

158
00:11:14,945 --> 00:11:20,022
The textbook says the analogy is exact
about the danger and incomplete about the

159
00:11:20,062 --> 00:11:25,266
timing. Exact about the danger: text
substituted into a script becomes part of the

160
00:11:25,306 --> 00:11:30,318
script. That's how an untrusted pull
request title becomes a shell command, and

161
00:11:30,358 --> 00:11:35,562
video 158 is about that. Incomplete about
the timing: not every field is available

162
00:11:35,602 --> 00:11:36,522
in every place.

163
00:11:36,862 --> 00:11:40,342
So for every expression ask:
who evaluates this, and when?

164
00:11:40,382 --> 00:11:41,798
There are three moments.

165
00:11:42,138 --> 00:11:47,000
Before a runner is assigned, the service
evaluates on, the job-level if, and the

166
00:11:47,040 --> 00:11:47,429
matrix.

167
00:11:47,769 --> 00:11:52,762
On the runner, per step, before the shell
starts, the step-level if, with, env, and

168
00:11:52,802 --> 00:11:55,612
any expression inside a
run text are evaluated.

169
00:11:55,952 --> 00:11:59,510
And at run time, the shell expands
an ordinary shell variable.

170
00:11:59,850 --> 00:12:03,339
The difference between the last
two is a security boundary.

171
00:12:03,379 --> 00:12:07,107
An expression inside run pastes
the value into the script text.

172
00:12:07,147 --> 00:12:12,071
A value mapped into env and then read as a
shell variable is handed to the shell as

173
00:12:12,111 --> 00:12:16,976
data. Every course workflow uses the second
form, including for values that aren't

174
00:12:17,016 --> 00:12:19,966
attacker-controlled, so
that the habit is uniform.

175
00:12:21,806 --> 00:12:26,955
Four rows, three questions: which commit,
which ref, and which copy of the workflow

176
00:12:26,995 --> 00:12:31,957
file. Look at the last column. Only push
and pull_request read the file from the

177
00:12:31,997 --> 00:12:33,645
commit the event refers to.

178
00:12:33,985 --> 00:12:38,723
And the second picture: three moments.
The service evaluates before a runner is

179
00:12:38,763 --> 00:12:43,319
assigned. The runner fills in expressions
per step, before the shell starts.

180
00:12:43,359 --> 00:12:46,464
And the shell expands its
own variables at run time.

181
00:12:48,304 --> 00:12:53,673
The situation: a pull request branch,
feature/reorder-report, that touches only

182
00:12:53,713 --> 00:12:58,465
Python files, while main moved on.
A Java workflow has the path filter

183
00:12:58,505 --> 00:12:59,560
java-service/**.

184
00:12:59,900 --> 00:13:02,400
Step 1: the pull request comparison.

185
00:13:02,740 --> 00:13:07,244
Three dots. Which files are listed, and
what's the exit status of the second

186
00:13:07,284 --> 00:13:08,738
command? Say it out loud.

187
00:13:13,578 --> 00:13:19,168
Two Python files. Exit status 0 from git
diff --quiet means no difference under that

188
00:13:19,208 --> 00:13:21,782
path. The Java workflow wouldn't start.

189
00:13:22,122 --> 00:13:24,332
Step 2: what two dots would say.

190
00:13:24,672 --> 00:13:29,109
Four files. stock.py and its test
changed on main, not on the branch.

191
00:13:29,449 --> 00:13:33,387
Two dots compare the two tips and
blame the branch for what main did.

192
00:13:33,427 --> 00:13:38,115
For a pull request GitHub is documented to
use three dots: the merge base compared

193
00:13:38,155 --> 00:13:40,651
with the head, which is
what the branch did.

194
00:13:40,991 --> 00:13:46,454
Try it now, thirty seconds, in a repository
of your own, on a branch other than the

195
00:13:46,494 --> 00:13:52,024
default one. Run git diff --name-only
origin/main...HEAD, with your default branch's

196
00:13:52,064 --> 00:13:55,538
name if it isn't main. Three
dots, and it only reads.

197
00:13:55,578 --> 00:13:57,129
What does the list show?

198
00:13:59,469 --> 00:14:04,282
It shows every path your branch changed
since the merge base, the commit where it

199
00:14:04,322 --> 00:14:08,956
left the default branch. For a pull
request, that's the list a paths filter is

200
00:14:08,996 --> 00:14:13,510
documented to be compared with. If
nothing in it matches, no run is created.

201
00:14:13,850 --> 00:14:15,329
Step 3: a push.

202
00:14:15,669 --> 00:14:20,536
git commit adds an object and moves the
branch. For a push, the comparison is two

203
00:14:20,576 --> 00:14:25,503
dots between the old and the new tip of
the pushed branch: the commits you pushed.

204
00:14:25,543 --> 00:14:27,563
Exit status? Make your prediction.

205
00:14:32,403 --> 00:14:37,662
Status 1: a path under java-service/
changed. The Java workflow would start.

206
00:14:38,002 --> 00:14:43,965
Step 4: the lab replay. Replay
labs/run ch20a/lab-26-5-path-filter.

207
00:14:44,005 --> 00:14:51,222
Here the filter has two entries: the Java
directory and the workflow file itself.

208
00:14:51,562 --> 00:14:57,031
A documentation branch. Only README.md
changed. Status 0: the workflow doesn't

209
00:14:57,071 --> 00:15:02,681
start. If that workflow were a required
check, this is the pull request from the

210
00:15:02,721 --> 00:15:03,034
hook.

211
00:15:03,374 --> 00:15:06,571
A second commit on the same
branch changes pom.xml.

212
00:15:06,611 --> 00:15:10,443
The three-dot diff now lists
both files, and the status is 1.

213
00:15:10,483 --> 00:15:15,521
Notice what this means for a pull request:
the comparison is over the whole pull

214
00:15:15,561 --> 00:15:20,663
request, so once any commit in it touches
a filtered path, the workflow starts on

215
00:15:20,703 --> 00:15:22,631
every later push to it as well.

216
00:15:22,971 --> 00:15:26,759
Lab 26.2 on your practice
repository, in your normal shell.

217
00:15:26,799 --> 00:15:32,145
The interface changes. The lab text and the
linked documentation are the reference.

218
00:15:32,185 --> 00:15:35,065
No GitHub output was
captured by the authors.

219
00:15:35,405 --> 00:15:41,062
Read the trigger of workflows/02-lint.yml
first: pushes to main, and pull requests.

220
00:15:41,102 --> 00:15:46,827
The lab has you add the workflow on a branch
named ci/lint, push the branch and open

221
00:15:46,867 --> 00:15:52,386
a pull request. git push is 🟡 CAUTION and
gh pr create is 🟡 CAUTION: it creates a

222
00:15:52,426 --> 00:15:54,788
GitHub object that notifies people.

223
00:15:55,128 --> 00:16:00,096
Before the push, the lab asks you to
predict: which workflows start for the pull

224
00:16:00,136 --> 00:16:04,917
request, and how many checks will it
show? Think about it with what you know.

225
00:16:04,957 --> 00:16:10,114
The push to ci/lint is a push, but not to
main, so the push trigger with its branch

226
00:16:10,154 --> 00:16:15,373
filter doesn't match. The pull_request
trigger matches once the pull request exists.

227
00:16:15,413 --> 00:16:20,319
And the lint workflow file is on the
branch, not yet on main: for pull_request,

228
00:16:20,359 --> 00:16:21,509
which copy is used?

229
00:16:21,849 --> 00:16:25,779
In the browser, open the pull
request and find the list of checks.

230
00:16:25,819 --> 00:16:30,833
Name each by the workflow and job it comes
from. Then look at two expressions in the

231
00:16:30,873 --> 00:16:35,826
file while the run is in progress: the if
with !cancelled() on the formatting step,

232
00:16:35,866 --> 00:16:40,819
which needs the wrapper because it starts
with an exclamation mark, and the summary

233
00:16:40,859 --> 00:16:45,030
step that maps two step outcomes into
env before the shell reads them.

234
00:16:46,870 --> 00:16:48,518
Five mistakes to watch for.

235
00:16:48,858 --> 00:16:51,816
A required check on a
workflow with a paths filter.

236
00:16:51,856 --> 00:16:56,694
Root cause: a workflow that a filter skips
never creates its check, and the ruleset

237
00:16:56,734 --> 00:16:58,928
waits for a result that will not come.

238
00:16:59,268 --> 00:17:02,674
Predicting a pull request path
filter with a two-dot diff.

239
00:17:02,714 --> 00:17:07,665
Root cause: for pull requests the comparison
is three dots, from the merge base; two

240
00:17:07,705 --> 00:17:10,339
dots include what changed
on the base branch.

241
00:17:10,679 --> 00:17:15,572
An if with text outside the expression
wrapper. Root cause: the whole value becomes

242
00:17:15,612 --> 00:17:19,673
a non-empty string, which is truthy,
so the condition is always true.

243
00:17:20,013 --> 00:17:25,122
A typing error in steps.<id>.outputs. Root
cause: a property that does not exist

244
00:17:25,162 --> 00:17:28,148
evaluates to an empty
string, without an error.

245
00:17:28,488 --> 00:17:32,811
Expecting a secret in a fork pull
request, or testing secrets in if.

246
00:17:32,851 --> 00:17:37,753
Root cause: secrets are not passed to
workflows triggered from forks, and the

247
00:17:37,793 --> 00:17:40,256
secrets context is not available in if.

248
00:17:42,096 --> 00:17:46,563
Now, out of the lab. A platform team
runs a nightly evaluation with schedule.

249
00:17:46,603 --> 00:17:51,364
An engineer improves the evaluation workflow
on a feature branch and waits for the

250
00:17:51,404 --> 00:17:56,164
night to see the result. In the morning
the run has used the old workflow, and the

251
00:17:56,204 --> 00:17:57,686
engineer suspects a cache.

252
00:17:58,026 --> 00:18:02,914
Read the table. For schedule, the commit
is the last commit on the default branch

253
00:18:02,954 --> 00:18:07,782
and the ref is the default branch. A
scheduled run knows nothing about a feature

254
00:18:07,822 --> 00:18:12,588
branch. To try the changed workflow before
merging, the engineer needs an event

255
00:18:12,628 --> 00:18:14,170
that's about a chosen ref.

256
00:18:14,510 --> 00:18:19,408
That is workflow_dispatch, with its own
condition: it works only if the workflow

257
00:18:19,448 --> 00:18:24,160
file exists on the default branch. So
the first version of a manually started

258
00:18:24,200 --> 00:18:29,222
workflow has to be merged before it can be
started by hand from any branch at all.

259
00:18:31,062 --> 00:18:35,475
Your turn. Do Lab 26.2,
"Workflow 2, linting", in

260
00:18:35,515 --> 00:18:41,019
lab-manual/m26-actions-fundamentals.md,
in your normal shell.

261
00:18:41,359 --> 00:18:46,204
Predict before step 3: which workflows
start for the pull request, and how many

262
00:18:46,244 --> 00:18:51,337
checks it will show. Then, in the failure
part of the lab, predict which step fails

263
00:18:51,377 --> 00:18:53,872
and whether the step after it still runs.

264
00:18:54,212 --> 00:19:00,995
The challenge is Exercise 26.4, "Will
the path filter start the workflow?", in

265
00:19:01,035 --> 00:19:04,319
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

266
00:19:08,159 --> 00:19:12,556
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
goes event by event and keeps the two

267
00:19:12,596 --> 00:19:16,759
questions separate, because they have
different answers for some events.

268
00:19:16,799 --> 00:19:21,546
For pull_request it names the commit exactly
and says why it isn't the branch tip.

269
00:19:21,586 --> 00:19:24,816
It mentions the condition
attached to workflow_dispatch.

270
00:19:24,856 --> 00:19:29,662
The follow-up is about a pull request that
edits its own workflow to remove a test:

271
00:19:29,702 --> 00:19:34,391
say whether the edited file runs, and then
what that implies about who may change

272
00:19:34,431 --> 00:19:38,185
workflow files and what a required
check can and can't guarantee.

273
00:19:40,025 --> 00:19:45,206
Let's land this. The waiting pull request
from the opening has its explanation: the

274
00:19:45,246 --> 00:19:49,923
filter created no run, so no check was
reported, and the rule kept waiting.

275
00:19:50,263 --> 00:19:51,923
You should now be able to say:

276
00:19:52,263 --> 00:19:57,108
Each event fixes a commit and a ref: the
pushed tip for push, the test merge under

277
00:19:57,148 --> 00:20:02,052
refs/pull/N/merge for pull_request, the
default branch for schedule, the chosen ref

278
00:20:02,092 --> 00:20:03,363
for workflow_dispatch.

279
00:20:03,703 --> 00:20:08,277
A filter decides whether a run is created
at all, and a run that is not created

280
00:20:08,317 --> 00:20:09,270
reports no check.

281
00:20:09,610 --> 00:20:14,204
Path filters use a three-dot diff for pull
requests and a two-dot diff for pushes,

282
00:20:14,244 --> 00:20:16,069
and you can compute both locally.

283
00:20:16,409 --> 00:20:21,550
An expression is replaced by its value before
the shell starts; which contexts exist

284
00:20:21,590 --> 00:20:22,722
depends on the key.

285
00:20:23,062 --> 00:20:27,966
env is in the file, vars and secrets are
in settings, only secrets are masked, and

286
00:20:28,006 --> 00:20:30,076
an unset secret is an empty string.

287
